WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.590
Now, this is our solution.

00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.160
First, Alice and
Bob agree publicly

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.000
on a prime modulus
and a generator.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.710
In this case, 17 and 3.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.050
Then Alice selects a
private random number,

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:21.120
say 15, and calculates 3
to the power 15 mod 17,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.940
and sends this result
publicly to Bob.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.860
Then Bob selects his
private random number,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.610
say 13, and calculates 3
to the power 13 mod 17,

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.710
and sends this result
publicly to Alice.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.820
And now the heart of the trick.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.430
Alice takes Bob's public
result and raises it

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.030
to the power of
her private number

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.440
to obtain the shared secret,
which in this case is 10.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.110
Bob takes Alice's public
result and raises it

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.640
to the power of
his private number,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.670
resulting in the
same shared secret.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:03.140
Notice they did the
same calculation,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.640
though it may not
look like it at first.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.030
Consider Alice.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.110
The 12 she received from
Bob was calculated as 3

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.120
to the power 13 mod 17.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.980
So her calculation was the
same as 3 to the power 13

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.110
to the power 15 mod 17.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.510
Now consider Bob.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:28.210
The 6 he received from Alice was
calculated as 3 to the power 15

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:33.510
mod 17, so his calculation was
the same as 3 to the power 15

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.090
to the power 13.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.980
Notice they did the same
calculation with the exponents

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.330
in a different order.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.670
When you flip the exponent,
the result doesn't change.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.670
So they both calculated
3 raised to the power

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.860
of their private numbers.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.980
Without one of these
private numbers, 15 or 13,

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.950
Eve will not be able
to find the solution.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.900
And this is how it's done.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.090
While Eve is stuck grinding
away at the discrete logarithm

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.900
problem, and with
large enough numbers,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.280
we can say it's practically
impossible for her

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.289
to break the encryption in
a reasonable amount of time.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:11.730
This solves the key
exchange problem.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.000
It can be used in conjunction
with a pseudorandom generator

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.900
to encrypt messages between
people who have never met.

